          "title": "6.4 Trade Bill Journal and Ledger Entries",
          "image": "https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/63e56114746188c54e2936e0/6781888a173054767b820c25_67818587463ffdbd34df4710_AccountsReceivableJournalEntryStep-by-stepProcess_AccountsReceivableJournalEntry.png",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EECBk4C_Jk8",
          "content": "While individual journal entries have been shown, businesses that deal with many bills of exchange might maintain specialized journals for them to streamline recording, similar to Sales or Purchases Journals.\n\n*   **Bills Receivable Journal:**\n    *   Used to record details of all bills receivable drawn and accepted.\n    *   Columns would typically include: Date, Debtor's Name (Drawee), Acceptor, Drawer (if different from payee), Term, Due Date, Amount, Ledger Folio for Debtor's Account.\n    *   The total of the \"Amount\" column is posted periodically (e.g., monthly):\n        *   **Debit:** Bills Receivable Control Account (in General Ledger).\n        *   Individual amounts are posted as **credits** to the respective Debtors' accounts in the Sales Ledger.\n\n*   **Bills Payable Journal:**\n    *   Used to record details of all bills payable accepted by the business.\n    *   Columns would typically include: Date, Creditor's Name (Drawer/Payee), Maker (if it's a Promissory Note issued by the business), Term, Due Date, Amount, Ledger Folio for Creditor's Account.\n    *   The total of the \"Amount\" column is posted periodically:\n        *   **Credit:** Bills Payable Control Account (in General Ledger).\n        *   Individual amounts are posted as **debits** to the respective Creditors' accounts in the Purchases Ledger.\n\n**Ledger Accounts related to Bills:**\n\n*   **Bills Receivable Account (Asset Account - in General Ledger):**\n    *   Debited when bills are received from debtors.\n    *   Credited when bills are honoured, discounted, endorsed, or dishonoured.\n    *   Normal balance is debit (representing bills currently held).\n\n*   **Bills Payable Account (Liability Account - in General Ledger):**\n    *   Credited when the business accepts bills drawn on it or issues promissory notes.\n    *   Debited when bills payable are honoured (paid) or dishonoured.\n    *   Normal balance is credit (representing bills accepted by the business that are outstanding).\n\n*   **Individual Debtor's and Creditor's Accounts (in Subsidiary Ledgers):**\n    *   These reflect the conversion of an open account balance into a bill and then its settlement or dishonour.\n\n*   **Discount Charges Account / Rebate on Bill (Expense - in General Ledger):** For discounts when discounting or retiring bills.\n*   **Discount Received Account / Rebate on Bill (Income - in General Ledger):** For rebates received when retiring bills.\n*   **Noting Charges Account (Expense - usually in acceptor's books if they become liable for it, or part of Bad Debts/Debtor's recovery for the drawer).**\n\n**Presentation in Financial Statements:**\n*   **Bills Receivable:** Shown as a Current Asset on the Statement of Financial Position (Balance Sheet), usually under \"Trade and Other Receivables\" or as a separate line item if material.\n*   **Bills Payable:** Shown as a Current Liability on the Statement of Financial Position, usually under \"Trade and Other Payables\" or as a separate line item if material.\n\nMastering the journal entries for various bill transactions from both the drawer's/payee's and drawee's/maker's perspectives is crucial for accurately accounting for these instruments.",
