          "content": "This section would focus on testing understanding of the processes, even if not fully executable in an exam.\n\n**Computerized Systems:**\n\nQuestions:\n\n\"Describe the steps you would take to enter a sales invoice for a new credit customer in a typical accounting software package.\"\n\n\"Explain how an aged debtors report is generated in a computerized system and why it is useful.\"\n\n\"What are three security features you would expect in an accounting software to protect against unauthorized access?\"\n\n\"Outline the process of bank reconciliation using an accounting software package.\"\n\nFocus: Understanding of menus, data input screens, automated posting, report generation, and control features (rather than actually using software).\n\n**Manual Systems:**\n\nQuestions (could be integrated into structured questions):\n\n\"Draw up a Sales Journal and post the total to the relevant general ledger accounts.\" (Often with simplified numbers).\n\n\"Prepare a three-column cash book extract for the following transactions...\"\n\n\"Explain the imprest system of petty cash.\"\n\n(The aim here is to test procedural understanding relevant to both contexts, aligning with WAEC's practical aspects emphasis where applicable.)",
